  "prompt": "A subnet is announced as `10.0.0.64/26`. How many TOTAL addresses does the block contain, and how many are USABLE for hosts?\n\n**A.** 64 total, 62 usable\n**B.** 26 total, 24 usable\n**C.** 64 total, 64 usable\n**D.** 128 total, 126 usable",
  "instructions": [
    "Choose the single best answer."
  ],
  "constraints": [],
  "options": [
    "64 total, 62 usable",
    "26 total, 24 usable",
    "64 total, 64 usable",
    "128 total, 126 usable"
  ],
  "correctOptionIndex": 0,
  "examples": [],
  "hints": [
    "How many bits remain after the prefix?",
    "Which two addresses are always reserved?"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "summary": "The correct answer is A — 64 total, 62 usable",
    "answer": "64 total, 62 usable",
    "reasoning": [
      "Host bits = 32 − prefix length = 6; total = 2⁶ = 64.",
      "The first address (10.0.0.64) is the network id, the last (10.0.0.127) is broadcast.",
      "Usable range: 10.0.0.65 – 10.0.0.126 = 62 hosts."
    ],
    "whyNot": [
      "26 total, 24 usable — The /26 prefix length is not the address count — host bits are 32 minus the prefix.",
      "64 total, 64 usable — Network and broadcast addresses are reserved, so two of the 64 are not assignable.",
      "128 total, 126 usable — That would be a /25 (7 host bits)."
    ]
  },
